OS is the core software that sits between the user, the apps and the hardware and manages all of them. You can think of it as air traffic control for your computer.

Privilege layers

Kernel space: where the kernel runs, with full access to the hardware.
User space: where apps run, with restricted access. To use hardware, an app has to ask the kernel through a system call. Because of this split, one bad app can’t crash the whole system.

Core duties

Process management: schedules programs and shares out CPU time.
Memory management: gives RAM to apps, keeps them isolated from each other, and uses virtual memory when RAM runs low.
File system management: handles folders, paths, permissions and metadata.
User management: handles accounts, logins and access rights.
Device management: uses drivers and a hardware abstraction layer so devices work with apps.

Security: authentication, permissions, process isolation and protection of system files.

Interfaces

GUI: visual, point and click, easy to use.
CLI: typed commands, more precise and faster, but you need to know the commands.

OS types

Desktop: Windows, macOS, Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora).
Server: headless (no screen) and built for uptime. Examples are Windows Server, Linux and Unix (AIX, Solaris).
Mobile: touch-based and power-efficient. Examples are Android and iOS.
Embedded/IoT: small and lightweight. Examples are OpenWrt and real-time OSes like FreeRTOS, VxWorks and QNX.
Virtual/Cloud: scalable and fast to deploy. Examples are Ubuntu LTS and Amazon Linux, plus Alpine and Bottlerocket for containers.

Why so many? Different devices need different things: ease of use, uptime, battery life or a small footprint.

